    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense regarding the throughput. I'm particularly interested in the \"friction\" aspect you mentioned. In a signalized system, there are often these unpredictable surges in delay or sudden, inexplicable stalls in flow that don't always align with the timing of the lights. What are the primary systemic causes for that kind of irregular, non-linear friction in heavy urban traffic?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The irregular friction in signalized systems often stems from queue spillback and upstream coordination failures. When one approach experiences an unforeseen bottleneck or saturation, the resulting queue can block downstream minor approaches, effectively sabotaging the timing plan. Furthermore, driver reaction times, especially during phase changes, combined with shockwaves propagating backward through the network, contribute significantly to these non-linear flow disruptions.",
      "source": "target",
