    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Right, so it's mostly a cascade effect from the initial bottleneck. But even when the volumes seem well within capacity, we still see these weird, localized spikes in friction—almost like the system is being interrupted by something small and chaotic that isn't captured in the heavy vehicle data. If we wanted to model these 'micro-disruptions' in the flow, what kind of stochastic variables should we be looking at to account for that sudden loss of efficiency?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To model these micro-disruptions, you should incorporate variables representing driver behavior and perception, such as headway variance and reaction time distributions. Including unsignalized pedestrian crossing behavior or sudden vehicle intrusions (like emergency vehicles or aggressive lane changes) as rare event generators can capture these chaotic inputs. Statistical models employing queuing theory with non-deterministic arrivals are best suited for quantifying this localized, unpredictable friction.",
      "source": "target",
